            "text": "{\"claims\":[{\"type\":\"domain\",\"location\":\"t ≥ T\",\"explanation\":\"The formal set counts time only after the second car starts. If the informal phrase “total amount of time” also includes the interval after the first car starts but before the second starts, both cars have completed zero laps until the first car finishes its first lap. That adds a positive interval not counted by the formal set.\",\"prediction\":\"Replacing t ≥ T with t ≥ 0 changes the asserted volume for some T > 0; the earlier interval contributes min(T, 1) hours.\",\"probe_kind\":\"difference\",\"comparison\":null,\"witnesses\":null,\"edit_from\":\"t ≥ T\",\"edit_to\":\"t ≥ 0\",\"bridge\":\"translated\",\"obligation\":\"Determine whether the intended problem counts times before the second car starts. If it considers only times when both cars are traveling, the formal restriction is appropriate.\"}],\"summary\":\"Possible time-domain mismatch: the formal proposition excludes the period between the cars’ start times.\"}"
